<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Billung March</h1><p class="st-meta">936 CE – 1066 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Saxon frontier march facing the Obotrites along the lower Elbe, held by the Billung family until the great Slavic revolt of 1066.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 936 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> entrusted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-billung">Hermann Billung</a> with command against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> along the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-holstein">Holstein</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peene">Peene</a>. This frontier region, later called the Billung March, covered roughly modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a> and eastern Holstein, inhabited mainly by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/obotrite-confederation">Obotrites</a> and other Wendish peoples who paid tribute.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The march was less a settled territory than a zone of military overlordship and tribute collection. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-billung">Billungs</a>, who also became dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, controlled it from strongholds along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>. Bishoprics such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">Oldenburg</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-holstein">Holstein</a> were founded to convert the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, but German control remained loose and the Obotrite princes kept considerable power.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-slav-rising">Great Slav Rising</a> of 983 shook German control, and after the murder of the Christian Obotrite prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottschalk-obotrite-prince">Gottschalk</a> in 1066 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> expelled German influence and destroyed churches. The march ceased to exist; German rule returned only with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> in the 12th century, leading to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Lordship of Mecklenburg</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/billung-march.jpg" alt="Billung March" width="320" height="412"><figcaption>Image: Alphathon /ˈæɫfə.θɒn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Billung_March_locator_map_(1004).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>936 CE – 1066 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hermann Billung, Bernhard I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Slavic paganism (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Saxon, Polabian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Marca Billingi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1001&amp;focus=billung-march" aria-label="Billung March on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4901 403 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Billung March lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5237 629h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5221 659h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5221 659h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Germany. <a href="/atlas?year=1001&amp;focus=billung-march">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

